# MobX testing

Narrows the common testing rules to what is specific about testing MobX
stores, actions, and reactions — test layout, runner, and framework
concerns for React components themselves still come from the `react`
pack's own `rules/testing.mdc`.

## Store tests are plain unit tests

- A store with no React dependency (no `observer`, no hook) is tested by
  constructing it directly and calling its actions/reading its computeds
  — no React Testing Library render is needed. Co-locate the test next to
  the store (`xyz.store.test.ts` beside `xyz.store.ts`), matching the
  project's existing test-file convention.
- Mock the store's injected dependencies (an API service passed into the
  constructor), not MobX itself — MobX's reactivity is the thing under
  test, not something to stub out.

## Awaiting async actions and reactions

- After calling an async action in a test, `await` the action's own
  returned promise (for a plain `async` method) or `await` a `when(...)`
  predicate on the resulting state (`await when(() => !store.loading)`)
  before asserting — do not assert immediately after the call and rely on
  a fixed `setTimeout`/sleep, which is both slower and flaky.
- When asserting on a value only a `reaction`/`autorun` sets, wait for
  that reaction to have run (`await when(() => condition)`, or `await
  Promise.resolve()` for microtask-only sync reactions) rather than
  asserting synchronously right after the triggering mutation.

## `enforceActions` in tests

- Keep `enforceActions` at the project's real (usually `"observed"` or
  `"always"`) setting in tests rather than relaxing it to `"never"` for
  convenience — a test that mutates observable state outside an action
  should fail the same way production code would, since that failure is
  exactly the bug class these tests exist to catch.
- If a test genuinely needs to seed store state directly (bypassing
  actions, e.g. to set up a precondition), wrap that seeding assignment
  in `runInAction` rather than disabling `enforceActions` for the whole
  suite.

## Testing disposal and reaction cleanup

- Assert that `dispose()` actually stops a store's reactions: trigger a
  mutation after `dispose()` and confirm the disposed reaction's effect
  did not run again — a leaked, undisposed `autorun`/`reaction` is a real
  bug that a naive "does dispose() throw" test will not catch.

## Component tests still need `observer`-aware setup

- A test rendering an `observer` component must trigger the store
  mutation the same way production code would (through the store's own
  action) and then assert on the rendered output after that action
  resolves — do not directly reassign an observable field the component
  reads and expect the same ordering guarantees an action provides.
